birds are known for their numerous ways  of communicating sound body language or  in one case a strange combination of  both  crusted pigeons are indigenous to  Australia aside from their wicked  Mohawks there doesn't seem to be  anything too special about them that is  until they take to the air when the  crested pigeon flaps its wings they  whistle with each wing beat the upstroke  produces a 1.3 kilohertz low note and  the down stroke produces a 2.9 kilohertz  I know  [Music]  while this whistle has been known for  some time scientists were curious was  this just a quirk of biology or a method  of communicating the distinctive sound  was discovered to be coming from the  eighth feather on the pigeons wing when  this feather was removed from the bird  the high note disappeared along with it  and when the feather was placed in a  wind tunnel the high-frequency sound was  heard once again when the researchers  played back audio recordings of slow  wing beats the birds stayed where they  were but when they played faster wing  beats the birds were more likely to try  and flee because the notes cycle back  and forth between high and low the speed  of the cycle informs the pigeons whether  there is danger present or if it's just  another bird taking off this discovery  means that even though the crusted  pigeon isn't much of a talker there are  more ways for birds to communicate  audibly not with their mouths but with  their movements  [Music]
 

